## Your rights at work

COPD often counts as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010**. Your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- moving you away from **dust, fumes and smoke**
- **less walking, lifting or climbing stairs**, or a ground floor workspace
- a **parking space** near the entrance
- **flexible hours** or working from home
- time off for **appointments and pulmonary rehab**

## Benefits

PIP is based on how COPD affects you on **most days**. Breathlessness and tiredness can affect many activities, such as:

- **moving around:** how far you can walk before you have to stop, reliably and repeatedly
- **washing and bathing:** if you need to sit, or steam and effort make you breathless
- **dressing:** if bending or lifting your arms leaves you breathless
- **preparing food:** if you cannot stand at the cooker or carry pans

| Your age | Benefit to look at |
| --- | --- |
| 16 to State Pension age | [Personal Independence Payment (PIP)](/benefits/pip/) |
| State Pension age or over | [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) |

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## Work-related lung disease

If your COPD was caused by your job, you may be able to claim **Industrial Injuries Disablement Benefit**. For example, COPD in **coal miners** is a recognised work-related disease. Other lung diseases caused by work may also qualify.

## Other help

- **Blue Badge:** you may be able to get one if you have great difficulty walking.
- **Keeping warm:** you may be able to get help with energy bills. Ask your council or Citizens Advice.
- **Equipment:** a council needs assessment can arrange aids such as a shower seat or perching stool.
